@page "/web-app"

<PageTitle>Blazor Web App · Belemetry</PageTitle>

<div class="bb-intro">
    <h1>Blazor Web App</h1>
    <p class="bb-subtitle">
        A Blazor Web App has two projects and up to four ways of rendering a page - Server, WebAssembly,
        Auto and static SSR. Belemetry is set up the same way for all of them: one registration in each
        project, <code>&lt;BelemetryScript /&gt;</code> in the document head, and
        <code>&lt;BelemetryInitializer /&gt;</code> in <code>Routes.razor</code>. What differs between the
        render modes is where the .NET half runs, and that is decided for you.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Install</h3>
    <p>
        One package, referenced by the project that renders <code>App.razor</code> (the server) and by
        the client project when there is one. It brings the browser script with it as a static web
        asset, at <code>_content/Bit.Belemetry/bit-belemetry.js</code>.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@InstallCode" Language="Shell" Expanded="true" />
    <p>
        Then import the namespace in <code>_Imports.razor</code> of every project whose components use it - the
        server's, for <code>App.razor</code>, and the client's, for <code>Routes.razor</code> and the pages:
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@ImportsCode" Language="Razor" Expanded="true" />
    <div class="bb-note bb-note-warn">
        <p>
            Without the <code>@@using</code>, <code>&lt;BelemetryScript /&gt;</code> compiles as an unknown HTML element -
            only a warning, no error - the page renders it as an empty tag, and telemetry silently never starts. If
            nothing arrives, view the page source and look for <code>bit-belemetry-config</code>.
        </p>
    </div>
    <p>What it adds to the download, measured on a trimmed Release publish (brotli, as served):</p>
    <div class="bb-scroll">
        <table class="bb-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th scope="col">Part</th><th scope="col">Size</th><th scope="col">Downloaded by</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>bit-belemetry.js</code> - the browser script</td><td>16 KB</td><td>Every page, in every render mode</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>Bit.Belemetry.wasm</code> - the .NET pipeline, both encoders, the bridge</td><td>107 KB</td><td>WebAssembly and Auto, with the rest of the app's assemblies</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>System.Diagnostics.DiagnosticSource.wasm</code></td><td>29 KB</td><td>The same, when the app did not already ship it</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p>
        <code>System.Text.Json</code> and <code>System.Net.Http</code> are part of every Blazor WebAssembly app already.
        The package is trimmable and AOT-compatible, and its own test suite fails on any trim or AOT warning raised
        inside it.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Register in both projects</h3>
    <p>
        Call <code>AddBitBelemetryServices</code> in the server's <code>Program.cs</code> and in the
        client's. Every component is instantiated twice - once on the server for the prerender pass and
        again in the browser - so whatever a page injects (<code>IBelemetry</code>,
        <code>IBelemetryConsent</code>) has to resolve in both containers. The call is idempotent and
        follows the Options pattern: every configure callback, across calls, runs in order on the same
        <code>BelemetryOptions</code>, which makes a shared configuration method the natural way to keep
        the two in step.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@SharedCode" Language="C#" Expanded="true" />
    <CodeSnippet Code="@ServerProgramCode" Language="C#" Expanded="true" />
    <CodeSnippet Code="@ClientProgramCode" Language="C#" Expanded="true" />

    <p>Each container has its own job:</p>
    <div class="bb-scroll">
        <table class="bb-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th scope="col">Container</th><th scope="col">What Belemetry does there</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td>Server</td>
                    <td>
                        Builds the browser script's configuration from <em>its</em> options and writes it
                        into the page with <code>&lt;BelemetryScript /&gt;</code> - so the exporters, the
                        consent defaults and every <code>options.Browser.*</code> setting the browser sees come
                        from the server registration. What it writes is public, so it never includes an
                        exporter's <code>Headers</code> - only its <code>BrowserHeaders</code> (see
                        <a href="/otlp">OTLP</a>). Resolves <code>IBelemetry</code> and
                        <code>IBelemetryConsent</code> during prerendering and for interactive Server
                        circuits. Collects the server's own .NET telemetry only if you opt in (below).
                    </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Client</td>
                    <td>
                        Runs the .NET pipeline in the browser: the WebAssembly app's <code>ILogger</code>,
                        <code>Activity</code> and <code>Meter</code> records are collected and exported from
                        there, and flushed with <code>keepalive</code> when the page unloads. Its metrics rely on
                        <code>System.Diagnostics.Metrics</code>, which the WebAssembly SDK turns off by default and the
                        Bit.Belemetry package turns back on (<code>MetricsSupport</code>). <code>HttpClient</code>
                        spans are a switch of their own that the package leaves off
                        (<code>HttpActivityPropagationSupport</code>): off, its requests go out through the
                        browser's <code>fetch</code> and the script records them as fetch spans - see
                        <a href="/dotnet">.NET instrumentation</a>.
                    </td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> The script in App.razor</h3>
    <p>
        <code>&lt;BelemetryScript /&gt;</code> renders the configuration as an inline JSON element and the
        module after it. Because it is static markup in the head, the script starts before Blazor boots -
        it sees the initial page view, the web vitals and errors thrown while the runtime downloads - and
        it also covers static SSR pages that never become interactive.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@AppRazorCode" Language="Razor" Expanded="true" />
    <div class="bb-note">
        <p>
            With <code>MapStaticAssets</code> and fingerprinted assets, pass the fingerprinted path:
            <code>&lt;BelemetryScript Src="@@Assets["_content/Bit.Belemetry/bit-belemetry.js"]" /&gt;</code>.
            Under a Content Security Policy, pass <code>Nonce</code>; it is added to both script elements.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> The initializer in Routes.razor</h3>
    <p>
        <code>&lt;BelemetryInitializer /&gt;</code> connects the script to .NET once the app is interactive:
        consent and the session flow both ways, browser records can be relayed to .NET, and the WebAssembly
        pipeline gets its unload flush. It renders nothing. Place it once, in <code>Routes.razor</code> or
        the main layout, inside the interactive part of the app.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@RoutesCode" Language="Razor" Expanded="true" />
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Per render mode</h3>
    <p>
        The setup above does not change between render modes. What changes is where .NET telemetry is
        collected and how <code>IBelemetry</code> calls travel:
    </p>
    <div class="bb-scroll">
        <table class="bb-table">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="col">Render mode</th>
                    <th scope="col">Browser instrumentation</th>
                    <th scope="col">.NET logs, traces, metrics</th>
                    <th scope="col"><code>IBelemetry</code> and consent</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td>InteractiveWebAssembly</td>
                    <td>The script, from the head</td>
                    <td>In the browser, exported by the client registration</td>
                    <td>Through the WebAssembly pipeline; consent is app-wide</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>InteractiveServer</td>
                    <td>The script, from the head</td>
                    <td>Opt-in: the server pipeline</td>
                    <td>Routed through the circuit's browser, so that user's consent applies</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>InteractiveAuto</td>
                    <td>The script, from the head - one script for the whole visit</td>
                    <td>On the server while a page runs on a circuit (opt-in), in the browser once Auto switches to WebAssembly</td>
                    <td>As Server, then as WebAssembly</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Static SSR</td>
                    <td>The script, from the head</td>
                    <td>Opt-in: the server pipeline</td>
                    <td>No interactivity: consent is given in the browser, through <code>window.BitBelemetry</code></td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p>
        On a static SSR page there is no circuit and no WebAssembly runtime, so
        <code>IBelemetryConsent</code> has nobody to talk to. The script is still running, and a plain
        HTML banner can record the choice with its JavaScript API - the same
        <code>bit-belemetry-consent</code> entry the .NET API writes, so an interactive page later reads
        the same decision:
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@StaticSsrConsentCode" Language="HTML" />
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> The server pipeline</h3>
    <p>
        On an ASP.NET Core host Belemetry's .NET pipeline is off by default - such hosts usually have an
        OpenTelemetry setup of their own - and the server registration only renders and serves the script.
        Turn it on to collect the server's logs, traces (including the <code>Microsoft.AspNetCore</code>
        request spans) and metrics with the same exporters. It is process-wide, serving every user at
        once, so it follows <code>Server.Features</code> - an operator decision - and not any one user's
        consent. With Server and Auto, this is also where an interactive circuit's <code>ILogger</code>
        output is collected.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@ServerPipelineCode" Language="C#" Expanded="true" />
    <p>
        Browser fetch spans add a <code>traceparent</code> to same-origin requests, so the server's
        request span becomes a child of the browser's span and the two land in one trace. More on the
        <a href="/server-pipeline">Server pipeline</a> page.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> What this site does</h3>
    <p>
        This documentation site is a Blazor Web App in the InteractiveWebAssembly render mode, prerendered,
        and it is set up exactly as above: <code>AddBitBelemetryServices</code> in both projects through a
        shared <code>DemoTelemetry.ConfigureShared</code>, <code>&lt;BelemetryScript /&gt;</code> in
        <code>App.razor</code>, <code>&lt;BelemetryInitializer /&gt;</code> in <code>Routes.razor</code>,
        and the server pipeline turned on. It exports OTLP/JSON to itself, and an in-process receiver
        hands each reader their own records back.
    </p>
    <div class="bb-note">
        <p>
            Answer the consent banner, then open <a href="/live">Live telemetry</a>: the page views, clicks,
            web vitals and .NET logs you produce on this site arrive there within a couple of seconds, with
            the demo's real configuration printed underneath.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> API</h3>
    <ApiTable TypeName="BelemetryScript" />
    <ApiTable TypeName="BelemetryInitializer" />
    <ApiTable TypeName="BelemetryServerOptions" />
</div>

@code {
    private const string InstallCode = """
        dotnet add package Bit.Belemetry
        """;

    private const string ImportsCode = """
        @* Server/Components/_Imports.razor and Client/_Imports.razor *@
        @using Bit.Belemetry
        """;

    private const string SharedCode = """
        // In the client project, so both Program.cs files can call it.
        using Bit.Belemetry;

        public static class AppTelemetry
        {
            public static void Configure(BelemetryOptions options)
            {
                options.ServiceName = "MyApp";
                options.AddOtlpExporter("https://otel.example.com");

                options.Consent.Default = BelemetryFeatures.None;   // opt-in until the user decides

                options.Traces.Sources.Add("MyApp*");
                options.Metrics.Meters.Add("MyApp");
            }
        }
        """;

    private const string ServerProgramCode = """
        // Server/Program.cs
        builder.Services.AddRazorComponents()
            .AddInteractiveServerComponents()
            .AddInteractiveWebAssemblyComponents();

        builder.Services.AddBitBelemetryServices(options =>
        {
            AppTelemetry.Configure(options);
            options.Environment = builder.Environment.EnvironmentName;
        });
        """;

    private const string ClientProgramCode = """
        // Client/Program.cs
        var builder = WebAssemblyHostBuilder.CreateDefault(args);

        builder.Services.AddBitBelemetryServices(options =>
        {
            AppTelemetry.Configure(options);
            options.Environment = builder.HostEnvironment.Environment;
        });

        await builder.Build().RunAsync();
        """;

    private const string AppRazorCode = """
        @* Server/Components/App.razor *@
        <!DOCTYPE html>
        <html lang="en">
        <head>
            <meta charset="utf-8" />
            <base href="/" />
            <BelemetryScript />
            <HeadOutlet @rendermode="InteractiveAuto" />
        </head>
        <body>
            <Routes @rendermode="InteractiveAuto" />
            <script src="_framework/blazor.web.js"></script>
        </body>
        </html>
        """;

    private const string RoutesCode = """
        @* Client/Routes.razor *@
        <Router AppAssembly="typeof(Program).Assembly">
            <Found Context="routeData">
                <RouteView RouteData="routeData" DefaultLayout="typeof(Layout.MainLayout)" />
            </Found>
        </Router>

        <BelemetryInitializer />
        """;

    private const string StaticSsrConsentCode = """
        <div id="consent">
            <button onclick="BitBelemetry.setConsent(BitBelemetry.Features.All)">Accept all</button>
            <button onclick="BitBelemetry.setConsent(BitBelemetry.Features.Diagnostics)">Diagnostics only</button>
            <button onclick="BitBelemetry.setConsent(BitBelemetry.Features.None)">Reject</button>
        </div>
        <script type="module">
            // window.BitBelemetry is set when the module loads; the choice persists in localStorage.
            if (window.BitBelemetry?.isConsentDecided()) document.getElementById('consent').remove();
        </script>
        """;

    private const string ServerPipelineCode = """
        builder.Services.AddBitBelemetryServices(options =>
        {
            AppTelemetry.Configure(options);

            options.Server.CollectDotnetTelemetry = true;
            options.Server.Features = BelemetryFeatures.Errors | BelemetryFeatures.Logs
                                    | BelemetryFeatures.Traces | BelemetryFeatures.Metrics;   // the default
        });
        """;
}
